        fp: "A commercial landlord entered into a written five-year commercial lease with an industrial tool retailer at $5,000 per month. Two years into the lease, the retailer suffered severe financial losses and vacated the building, returning the keys to the landlord with a letter stating that it was terminating the lease and would pay no further rent. The landlord accepted the keys, immediately listed the property with a licensed commercial real estate broker, and advertised the space at the fair market rental rate of $5,000 per month. Despite these diligent and reasonable efforts, the building remained vacant for four months before the landlord secured a replacement tenant who executed a three-year lease at $5,000 per month. The landlord sued the original retailer for $20,000 in lost rent for the four-month vacancy period. The retailer argued that the landlord's retention of the keys constituted an acceptance of surrender that extinguished all liability for rent.",
