        exp: "Rule: In a pure comparative negligence jurisdiction, a plaintiff's contributory fault does not completely bar recovery. Instead, the plaintiff's damages are reduced in direct proportion to the percentage of fault attributed to the plaintiff by the trier of fact. Here, the customer suffered $50,000 in damages and was found 40% at fault. His recovery is reduced by 40% ($20,000), leaving an enforceable award of $30,000 against the boutique (Option C). Option A is incorrect because pure comparative fault abolishes the traditional common law rule that any contributory negligence completely bars recovery. Option B is incorrect because recovery is reduced by the plaintiff's own percentage of fault, not by the differential between the parties' fault shares. Option D is incorrect because business owners are not strictly liable insurers; the plaintiff's comparative fault operates to reduce the total award."
